(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Electromagnetics & Antennas"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Electromagnetics & Antennas"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Electromagnetics & Antennas: Assignments\n\nThree assignments, seven questions each (21 in all), with complete solutions at the end of each assignment. All numerical answers were computed with Python (numpy and scipy, including numerical integration of antenna patterns); use c = 2.998 × 10^8 m/s and eta0 = 376.7 Ω.\n\n# Assignment 1: Plane Waves, Skin Effect and Reflection (Chapter 1)\n\n**Q1. Plane-wave parameters.** (a) The free-space wavelength at 1 MHz, 100 MHz, 1 GHz and 10 GHz [4] (b) For a lossless dielectric with epsilon_r = 4 and mu_r = 1: the velocity, intrinsic impedance and the wavelength at 1 GHz [4] (c) The average power density of a wave with a peak electric field of 10 V/m in free space [2]\n\n**Q2. Skin depth.** For copper (sigma = 5.8 × 10^7 S/m, mu = mu0) find the skin depth at 60 Hz, 1 MHz, 1 GHz and 10 GHz, and the surface resistance at 1 GHz [6]; the skin depth of seawater (sigma = 4 S/m) at 1 MHz and 1 kHz [3]; explain what this means for submarine communication [1]\n\n**Q3. Reflection at a dielectric.** (a) Gamma, tau and the fraction of power reflected for air to a dielectric with epsilon_r = 4 [4] (b) The same for air to glass (n = 1.5) and for air to water (n = 1.33) [4] (c) Explain anti-reflection coatings [2]\n\n**Q4. Brewster and total reflection.** Find the Brewster angle and the critical angle for glass (n = 1.5) and for water (n = 1.33), and explain the use of total internal reflection [10]\n\n**Q5. Field strength from an antenna.** A transmitter with an EIRP of 1 kW is observed at 100 m. Find the power density and the rms electric field [5]; what EIRP produces an rms field of 10 V/m at 10 m? [5]\n\n**Q6. Boundary conditions.** (a) State the boundary conditions for E and H at an interface and at a perfect conductor [5] (b) What is the reflection coefficient of a perfect conductor and what field pattern results? [5]\n\n**Q7. Polarisation.** (a) Define linear, circular and elliptical polarisation [4] (b) Why is circular polarisation used for satellites? [3] (c) What is the loss when a vertical and a horizontal linear antenna are used together? [3]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 1\n\n**Q1.** (a) lambda = c/f with c = 2.998 × 10^8 m/s:\n1 MHz: 2.998 × 10^8/10^6 = **299.8 m**. 100 MHz: 2.998 × 10^8/10^8 = **2.998 m**. 1 GHz: **29.98 cm**. 10 GHz: **2.998 cm**.\n(b) Step 1: v = c/sqrt(epsilon_r mu_r) = c/sqrt 4 = **1.5 × 10^8 m/s**.\nStep 2: eta = eta0/sqrt(epsilon_r) = 376.7/2 = **188.4 Ω**.\nStep 3: lambda = v/f = 1.5 × 10^8/10^9 = **15.0 cm**.\n(c) Step 1: S = E0²/(2 eta0) = 10²/(2 × 376.7) = 100/753.4 = **0.133 W/m²**.\nStep 2: check: E_rms = 10/sqrt 2 = 7.07 V/m and S = E_rms²/eta0 = 50/376.7 = 0.133 ✓.\n\n**Q2.** delta = 1/sqrt(pi f mu sigma) with mu = 4π × 10^-7 H/m.\nCopper (sigma = 5.8 × 10^7 S/m):\n60 Hz: pi f mu sigma = π × 60 × 4π × 10^-7 × 5.8 × 10^7 = 13,740, so delta = 1/sqrt 13,740 = **8.53 mm**.\n1 MHz: delta = **66.1 μm**. 1 GHz: delta = 66.1 μm/sqrt 1000 = **2.09 μm**. 10 GHz: **0.66 μm** (delta scales as 1/sqrt f).\nSurface resistance at 1 GHz: Rs = 1/(sigma delta) = 1/(5.8 × 10^7 × 2.09 × 10^-6) = 1/121.2 = **8.25 mΩ per square**.\nSeawater (sigma = 4 S/m): 1 MHz: delta = 1/sqrt(π × 10^6 × 4π × 10^-7 × 4) = 1/sqrt(15.79) = 1/3.974 = **0.25 m**. 1 kHz: 0.25 × sqrt 1000 = **7.96 m**.\nSeawater absorbs radio waves strongly (skin depth only a fraction of a metre at 1 MHz), so submerged submarines can be reached only by very low frequencies (tens of Hz to a few kHz), where the skin depth is metres to tens of metres, at tiny data rates.\n\n**Q3.** (a) Step 1: eta2 = 376.7/2 = 188.4 Ω; Gamma = (eta2 - eta1)/(eta2 + eta1) = (188.4 - 376.7)/(188.4 + 376.7) = -188.3/565.1 = **-0.333**.\nStep 2: tau = 1 + Gamma = **0.667**.\nStep 3: reflected power fraction = Gamma² = **11.1 percent**.\n(b) Glass: eta2 = 376.7/1.5 = 251.2 Ω; Gamma = (251.2 - 376.7)/(251.2 + 376.7) = -125.5/627.9 = **-0.2**; Gamma² = **4.0 percent** reflected (96 percent transmitted).\nWater: eta2 = 376.7/1.33 = 283.2 Ω; Gamma = (283.2 - 376.7)/(283.2 + 376.7) = **-0.1416**; Gamma² = **2.0 percent**.\n(c) A quarter-wave layer of a material with refractive index sqrt(n1 n2) between the two media causes the reflections from its two surfaces to arrive in antiphase and cancel (at the design wavelength): used on lenses, solar cells and radomes.\n\n**Q4.** Brewster angle (parallel polarisation: no reflection): theta_B = arctan(n2/n1).\nStep 1: glass: arctan 1.5 = **56.3°**. Water: arctan 1.33 = **53.1°**.\nCritical angle (from the dense to the less dense medium): theta_c = arcsin(n2/n1).\nStep 2: glass to air: arcsin(1/1.5) = arcsin 0.667 = **41.8°**. Water to air: arcsin(1/1.33) = **48.8°**.\n**Total internal reflection** keeps the light inside an optical fibre: the core has a higher index than the cladding, so rays that strike the boundary beyond the critical angle are completely reflected and travel hundreds of kilometres with little loss; also used in prisms and endoscopes.\n\n**Q5.** Step 1: power density S = EIRP/(4π R²) = 1000/(4π × 10⁴) = 1000/125,664 = **7.96 mW/m²**.\nStep 2: E_rms = sqrt(S eta0) = sqrt(7.96 × 10⁻³ × 376.7) = sqrt 3.0 = **1.73 V/m**.\nFor E_rms = 10 V/m at 10 m:\nStep 3: S = E²/eta0 = 100/376.7 = 0.2655 W/m².\nStep 4: EIRP = S × 4π R² = 0.2655 × 4π × 100 = 0.2655 × 1256.6 = **334 W**.\n\n**Q6.** (a) At an interface between two media: the **tangential E** is continuous, the **tangential H** is continuous (when there is no surface current; a surface current K gives a jump of H equal to K), the **normal D** is continuous (no surface charge; a surface charge rho_s gives a jump equal to rho_s) and the **normal B** is continuous. At a **perfect conductor**: tangential E = 0 and normal B = 0 (the fields inside are zero); the tangential H equals the surface current density and the normal D equals the surface charge density. (b) For a perfect conductor eta2 = 0, so **Gamma = -1**: total reflection with a 180° phase change of E. The incident and reflected waves form a **standing wave**: E = 2 E0 sin(beta z) with nulls at the conductor and every half-wavelength (a node at z = 0, n lambda/2), the basis of cavity resonators and shielded enclosures.\n\n**Q7.** (a) **Linear**: E oscillates along a fixed line (vertical or horizontal). **Circular**: two orthogonal components of equal amplitude with a 90° phase difference: the E vector rotates at constant magnitude (right-hand or left-hand). **Elliptical**: the general case of unequal amplitudes or phase differences other than 0°, 90° or 180°. (b) A satellite signal passes the ionosphere, where **Faraday rotation** rotates the plane of a linearly polarised wave by an unknown angle, and the orientation between the satellite and the ground antenna changes; circular polarisation is insensitive to the rotation so no polarisation mismatch loss occurs (GPS, satellite TV). (c) Cross-polarised linear antennas (vertical against horizontal) ideally receive no signal (**infinite loss**, polarisation efficiency cos^2(90°) = 0); for an angle psi between them the polarisation loss factor is cos^2(psi) (a 45° mismatch loses 3 dB); a linear antenna receiving a circularly polarised wave loses 3 dB.\n\n# Assignment 2: Transmission Lines and Waveguides (Chapters 2 and 3)\n\n**Q1. Line parameters.** (a) A line has L' = 250 nH/m and C' = 100 pF/m: Z0, velocity and the fraction of c [4] (b) The impedance of a coaxial cable with a = 0.45 mm, b = 1.47 mm and polyethylene dielectric (epsilon_r = 2.25) [3] (c) At 1 GHz the wavelength in a line with v = 0.667 c, and the length beyond which it must be treated as a transmission line [3]\n\n**Q2. Reflection and VSWR.** For a 50 Ω line and loads 100 Ω, 25 Ω, 75 Ω and 50 + j50 Ω find |Gamma|, VSWR, return loss and mismatch loss [10]\n\n**Q3. Power.** Ten watts are sent into a 100 Ω load on a 50 Ω line. Find the reflected and delivered powers, the return loss and what VSWR means in terms of the voltage maxima and minima [10]\n\n**Q4. Input impedance.** For Z0 = 50 Ω find Z_in for (a) Z_L = 100 Ω at lambda/4, lambda/8 and lambda/2 [5] (b) a short circuit at lambda/8 and lambda/4 [3] (c) Z0 = 75 Ω, Z_L = 150 Ω at lambda/8 [2]\n\n**Q5. Matching.** (a) Design quarter-wave transformers for 50 Ω to 100 Ω, 75 Ω to 300 Ω and 50 Ω to 200 Ω [4] (b) A load 100 + j50 Ω on a 50 Ω line is matched with a shorted shunt stub: give the two solutions (distance from the load and stub length) [4] (c) Name two broadband matching methods [2]\n\n**Q6. Rectangular waveguide.** For WR-90 (a = 22.86 mm, b = 10.16 mm) compute the cutoff of TE10, TE20 and TE01 and, at 8, 10 and 12 GHz, lambda_g, v_p/c, v_g/c and the TE10 wave impedance [10]\n\n**Q7. Waveguide design.** (a) A guide has a = 2.5 cm: the TE10 cutoff and lambda_g at 8 GHz [4] (b) Why can v_p exceed c? [3] (c) Compare waveguide with coaxial cable [3]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 2\n\n**Q1.** (a) Step 1: Z0 = sqrt(L'/C') = sqrt(250 × 10⁻⁹/(100 × 10⁻¹²)) = sqrt 2500 = **50 Ω**.\nStep 2: v = 1/sqrt(L'C') = 1/sqrt(250 × 10⁻⁹ × 100 × 10⁻¹²) = 1/sqrt(2.5 × 10⁻¹⁷) = **2.0 × 10⁸ m/s = 0.667 c**.\n(b) Z0 = (60/sqrt(epsilon_r)) ln(b/a) = (60/1.5) ln(1.47/0.45) = 40 × ln 3.267 = 40 × 1.184 = **47.4 Ω**.\n(c) Step 1: lambda = v/f = 0.667 × 3 × 10⁸/10⁹ = **20.0 cm**.\nStep 2: the line must be treated as a transmission line when its length exceeds about lambda/10 = **2 cm** (for example a PCB trace of 2 cm at 1 GHz).\n\n**Q2.** Formulas: Gamma = (Z_L - 50)/(Z_L + 50); VSWR = (1 + |Gamma|)/(1 - |Gamma|); RL = -20 log10 |Gamma|; ML = -10 log10(1 - |Gamma|²).\n**100 Ω**: Gamma = 50/150 = 1/3; VSWR = (4/3)/(2/3) = **2.00**; RL = 20 log 3 = **9.54 dB**; ML = -10 log(1 - 1/9) = **0.51 dB**.\n**25 Ω**: Gamma = -25/75 = -1/3: VSWR **2.00**; RL 9.54 dB; ML 0.51 dB (same magnitude: a load r times or 1/r times Z0 gives the same VSWR).\n**75 Ω**: Gamma = 25/125 = 0.2; VSWR = 1.2/0.8 = **1.50**; RL = 20 log 5 = **13.98 dB**; ML = -10 log(1 - 0.04) = **0.18 dB**.\n**50 + j50 Ω**: Gamma = j50/(100 + j50) = 0.2 + j0.4; |Gamma| = sqrt(0.04 + 0.16) = **0.447** at 63.4°; VSWR = 1.447/0.553 = **2.62**; RL = **6.99 dB**; ML = -10 log(1 - 0.2) = **0.97 dB**.\nStep 1: **100 Ω**: Gamma = 1/3: VSWR **2.00**; RL **9.54 dB**; ML **0.51 dB**.\nStep 2: **25 Ω**: Gamma = -1/3: VSWR **2.00**; RL 9.54 dB; ML 0.51 dB (same magnitude: a load r times or 1/r times Z0 gives the same VSWR).\nStep 3: **75 Ω**: Gamma = 0.2: VSWR **1.50**; RL **13.98 dB**; ML **0.18 dB**.\nStep 4: **50 + j50 Ω**: Gamma = j50/(100 + j50): |Gamma| = **0.447** at 63.4°; VSWR **2.62**; RL **6.99 dB**; ML **0.97 dB**.\n\n**Q3.** Step 1: Gamma = (100 - 50)/(100 + 50) = 1/3.\nStep 2: reflected power = 10 × (1/3)² = **1.11 W**; delivered = 10 - 1.11 = **8.89 W** (mismatch loss 10 log(10/8.89) = 0.51 dB).\nStep 3: return loss = 20 log10(3) = **9.54 dB**.\nStep 4: VSWR = (1 + 1/3)/(1 - 1/3) = **2**: the ratio of the maximum to the minimum voltage on the line is 2 (V_max = V+ (1 + |Gamma|), V_min = V+ (1 - |Gamma|)); the line has voltage maxima every half-wavelength, with minima a quarter-wavelength from them.\n\n**Q4.** Z_in = Z0 (Z_L + j Z0 tan(beta l))/(Z0 + j Z_L tan(beta l)), with beta l = 90° for lambda/4, 45° for lambda/8, 180° for lambda/2.\n(a) Z_L = 100 Ω, Z0 = 50 Ω:\nlambda/4: tan 90° is infinite, so Z_in = Z0²/Z_L = 2500/100 = **25 Ω**.\nlambda/8: tan 45° = 1: Z_in = 50 (100 + j50)/(50 + j100) = 50 (100 + j50)(50 - j100)/(50² + 100²) = 50 (10,000 - j7500)/12,500 = **40 - j30 Ω**.\nlambda/2: tan 180° = 0: Z_in = Z_L = **100 Ω** (the line repeats the load every half-wavelength).\n(b) Short circuit (Z_L = 0): lambda/8: Z_in = j Z0 tan 45° = **+j50 Ω** (inductive); lambda/4: tan 90° is infinite, so Z_in is **infinite (open circuit)** (a quarter-wave shorted stub is a high impedance, used as an insulator or \"metallic support\").\n(c) Z0 = 75, Z_L = 150, lambda/8: Z_in = 75 (150 + j75)/(75 + j150) = 75 (150 + j75)(75 - j150)/(75² + 150²); the product (150 + j75)(75 - j150) = 11,250 - j22,500 + j5625 + 11,250 = 22,500 - j16,875, so Z_in = 75 (22,500 - j16,875)/28,125 = **60 - j45 Ω**.\n\n**Q5.** (a) Quarter-wave transformer: Z1 = sqrt(Z0 Z_L), each section lambda/4 long at the design frequency:\n50 Ω to 100 Ω: sqrt(5000) = **70.7 Ω**. 75 Ω to 300 Ω: sqrt(22,500) = **150 Ω**. 50 Ω to 200 Ω: sqrt(10,000) = **100 Ω**.\n(b) Single shunt stub: load 100 + j50 Ω on 50 Ω.\nStep 1: z_L = 2 + j1; y_L = 1/(2 + j) = 0.4 - j0.2.\nStep 2: moving a distance d toward the generator, y(d) = (y_L + j t)/(1 + j y_L t) with t = tan(2π d/λ). Its real part equals 1 when t = 3 or t = -1.\nStep 3: t = 3: **d = 0.1988 lambda**; y = 1 + j1.00. A shorted stub must supply -j1.00: y_stub = -j cot(β l) = -j gives cot(β l) = 1, so β l = 45° and **l = 0.125 lambda**.\nStep 4: t = -1: **d = 0.375 lambda**; y = 1 - j1.00. The stub must supply +j1: cot(β l) = -1, so β l = 135° and **l = 0.375 lambda**.\nThe shorter set (the first) is preferred (less bandwidth narrowing).\n(c) **Multi-section (binomial or Chebyshev) transformers**, **tapered lines** (exponential, Klopfenstein), and lumped networks with several elements.\n\n**Q6.** Step 1: cutoff f_c = (c/2) sqrt((m/a)² + (n/b)²): TE10: c/(2a) = 2.998 × 10⁸/(2 × 0.02286) = **6.557 GHz**; TE20: c/a = **13.114 GHz**; TE01: c/(2b) = 2.998 × 10⁸/(2 × 0.01016) = **14.754 GHz**.\nStep 2: define r = sqrt(1 - (f_c/f)²) and use lambda_g = lambda/r, v_p = c/r, v_g = c r, Z = eta0/r.\nStep 3: **8 GHz**: r = sqrt(1 - 0.6717) = 0.573; lambda = 37.5 mm; lambda_g = 37.5/0.573 = **65.4 mm**; v_p/c = **1.746**; v_g/c = **0.573**; Z = 376.7/0.573 = **657.6 Ω**.\nStep 4: **10 GHz**: r = 0.755; lambda_g = 30.0/0.755 = **39.7 mm**; v_p/c **1.324**; v_g/c **0.755**; Z = **499.0 Ω**.\nStep 5: **12 GHz**: r = 0.837; lambda_g = 25.0/0.837 = **29.8 mm**; v_p/c **1.194**; v_g/c **0.838**; Z = **449.8 Ω**.\nCheck: v_p v_g = c² is satisfied (1.746 × 0.573 = 1).\n\n**Q7.** (a) Step 1: f_c = c/(2a) = 2.998 × 10⁸/(2 × 0.025) = **5.996 GHz**.\nStep 2: at 8 GHz: lambda = c/f = 3.747 cm.\nStep 3: lambda_g = lambda/sqrt(1 - (f_c/f)²) = 3.747/sqrt(1 - (5.996/8)²) = 3.747/sqrt(1 - 0.5617) = 3.747/0.662 = **5.66 cm**.\n(b) The phase velocity is the speed of the phase pattern along the guide (the wavefronts' intersection with the wall moves faster than the wave itself); no energy or information travels at v_p. The energy and information move at the **group velocity v_g = c²/v_p < c**, so relativity is not violated.\n(c) Waveguide: very low loss, very high power capability, no radiation or interference, but bulky, expensive, with a cutoff (no DC or low frequencies) and a limited single-mode band. Coaxial cable: broadband from DC, flexible and cheap, but higher loss (rising with frequency) and lower power limits; waveguides are used above about 3 to 100 GHz for radar, satellite feeds and high-power applications.\n\n# Assignment 3: Antennas and Wireless Links (Chapters 4 and 5)\n\n**Q1. Half-wave dipole.** (a) The physical length of a half-wave dipole at 100 MHz, 900 MHz and 2.4 GHz [3] (b) Its radiation resistance, directivity (linear and dBi) and half-power beamwidth [3] (c) The gain if the loss resistance is 5 Ω, and the effective aperture at 1 GHz [4]\n\n**Q2. Short dipole.** (a) The radiation resistance of a short dipole of length lambda/10 and lambda/50 [4] (b) The directivity of a Hertzian dipole [2] (c) Why are electrically small antennas inefficient and narrowband? [4]\n\n**Q3. Dish antenna.** (a) A 1 m dish at 10 GHz with 60 percent aperture efficiency: gain (linear and dBi), beamwidth and effective aperture [5] (b) A 2 m dish at 12 GHz with 55 percent efficiency: the gain, HPBW and the far-field distance for the 1 m dish at 10 GHz [5]\n\n**Q4. Friis.** (a) A 2.4 GHz link of 100 m, 20 dBm transmit power, 2.15 dBi antennas at both ends: the received power [4] (b) A geostationary downlink at 4 GHz, R = 36,000 km, P_t = 60 dBm, G_t = 40 dBi, G_r = 35 dBi: the received power [4] (c) A 5.8 GHz link of 100 m, 10 dBm, 3 dBi antennas: the free-space loss and received power [2]\n\n**Q5. Arrays.** (a) The directivity of uniform broadside arrays of 2, 4, 8 and 16 isotropic elements spaced lambda/2 [4] (b) Explain beam steering by phase shifts [3] (c) What are grating lobes? [3]\n\n**Q6. Zones and exposure.** (a) The far-field distance 2 D^2/lambda for the 1 m dish at 10 GHz and for a half-wave dipole [4] (b) The power density and electric field at 100 m for an EIRP of 1 kW [3] (c) The radio horizon for antennas at 30 m and 2 m [3]\n\n**Q7. Link design and EMC.** (a) Compute the free-space loss for 10 km at 900 MHz and the received power for P_t = 30 dBm, G_t = 10 dBi, G_r = 0 dBi [5] (b) List five EMC design practices [5]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 3\n\n**Q1.** (a) l = lambda/2 = c/(2f): 100 MHz: 2.998 × 10⁸/(2 × 10⁸) = **1.50 m**; 900 MHz: **16.7 cm**; 2.4 GHz: **6.25 cm** (in practice about 5 percent shorter because of end effects).\n(b) R_r = **73.1 Ω**; D = **1.641 (2.15 dBi)** (numerical integration of the pattern [cos((π/2) cos θ)/sin θ]²); HPBW = **78.1°**.\n(c) Step 1: efficiency eta = R_r/(R_r + R_loss) = 73/(73 + 5) = 0.936.\nStep 2: G = eta D = 1.641 × 0.936 = 1.536 = **1.86 dBi**.\nStep 3: effective aperture A_e = lambda² D/(4π) at 1 GHz (lambda = 0.2998 m) for the lossless dipole: 0.0899 × 1.641/12.566 = **0.0117 m²** (117 cm²).\n\n**Q2.** (a) R_r = 80π² (l/lambda)² = 789.6 (l/lambda)²:\nl = lambda/10: 789.6 × 0.01 = **7.9 Ω**. l = lambda/50: 789.6 × 0.0004 = **0.316 Ω**.\n(b) **D = 1.5 (1.76 dBi)**.\n(c) The radiation resistance falls with the square of the electrical size while the loss resistance (conductor and matching losses) stays about the same, so the efficiency falls; the reactance of a short antenna is large (capacitive), so the antenna has a high Q and a narrow bandwidth (Chu-Wheeler limit: the bandwidth is limited by the volume of the smallest sphere enclosing the antenna).\n\n**Q3.** (a) Step 1: lambda = c/f = 2.998 × 10⁸/10¹⁰ = 3.0 cm.\nStep 2: G = eta_a (π D/lambda)² = 0.6 × (π × 1/0.02998)² = 0.6 × 10,981 = **6589 (38.2 dBi)**.\nStep 3: HPBW ≈ 70 lambda/D = 70 × 0.03/1 = **2.1°**.\nStep 4: A_e = eta_a π D²/4 = 0.6 × 0.7854 = **0.471 m²** (check: lambda² G/(4π) = 0.0009 × 6589/12.566 = 0.471 m²).\n(b) Step 1: lambda = 2.5 cm at 12 GHz; G = 0.55 × (π × 2/0.025)² = 0.55 × 63,165 = **34,790 (45.4 dBi)**.\nStep 2: HPBW = 70 × 0.025/2 = **0.87°**.\nStep 3: far-field distance of the 1 m dish at 10 GHz: 2D²/lambda = 2/0.02998 = **66.7 m**.\n\n**Q4.** Friis in dB: P_r = P_t + G_t + G_r - FSPL, with FSPL = 20 log10(4π R/lambda).\n(a) Step 1: lambda = 2.998 × 10⁸/(2.4 × 10⁹) = 0.1249 m.\nStep 2: FSPL = 20 log10(4π × 100/0.1249) = 20 log10(10,060) = **80.0 dB** (equivalently 20 log10(0.1) + 20 log10(2400) + 32.44).\nStep 3: P_r = 20 + 2.15 + 2.15 - 80.0 = **-55.7 dBm**.\n(b) Step 1: lambda = 0.07495 m at 4 GHz; FSPL = 20 log10(4π × 3.6 × 10⁷/0.07495) = 20 log10(6.04 × 10⁹) = **195.6 dB**.\nStep 2: P_r = 60 + 40 + 35 - 195.6 = **-60.6 dBm**.\n(c) Step 1: FSPL = 20 log10(0.1) + 20 log10(5800) + 32.44 = -20 + 75.27 + 32.44 = **87.7 dB**.\nStep 2: P_r = 10 + 3 + 3 - 87.7 = **-71.7 dBm**.\n\n**Q5.** (a) Uniform broadside arrays with lambda/2 spacing of isotropic elements have D = N (numerical integration): N = 2, 4, 8, 16 give **2, 4, 8, 16** (10 log N = 3.0, 6.0, 9.0, 12.0 dB): doubling the number of elements doubles the directivity (+3 dB) and narrows the beam.\n(b) Applying a progressive phase delta between elements moves the direction of the main beam to cos(theta_0) = -delta lambda/(2π d): **electronic steering** without moving parts (phased-array radar, 5G massive MIMO, satellite terminals); adjusting amplitudes controls the side-lobe level (tapering).\n(c) For element spacing larger than lambda (or lambda/2 with a large steering angle) the array factor repeats and additional main beams (grating lobes) appear in unwanted directions; avoid by spacing below lambda/(1 + |sin theta_max|).\n\n**Q6.** (a) 1 m dish at 10 GHz: 2D²/lambda = 2 × 1/0.02998 = **66.7 m**; half-wave dipole (D = lambda/2): 2 (lambda/2)²/lambda = lambda/2 = **1.5 cm** at 10 GHz.\n(b) S = 1000/(4π × 100²) = **7.96 mW/m²**; E_rms = sqrt(S eta0) = sqrt(7.96 × 10⁻³ × 376.7) = **1.73 V/m**.\n(c) Radio horizon d = 4.12 (sqrt(h1) + sqrt(h2)) km = 4.12 (sqrt 30 + sqrt 2) = 4.12 (5.48 + 1.41) = **28.4 km**.\n\n**Q7.** (a) Step 1: FSPL = 20 log10(10 km) + 20 log10(900 MHz) + 32.44 = 20 + 59.08 + 32.44 = **111.5 dB**.\nStep 2: P_r = 30 + 10 + 0 - 111.5 = **-71.5 dBm** (28.5 dB above a -100 dBm receiver sensitivity).\n(b) Five EMC practices: keep the ground plane continuous and signal return paths short; shield sensitive circuits and cables (skin depth: thin metal suffices at RF); filter power and signal lines (ferrite beads, LC filters) at connectors; use differential signalling, twisted pairs and balanced lines to cancel pickup; slow the edges (limit rise time) and separate noisy digital from sensitive analog circuits; decouple supplies with capacitors close to the pins; provide ESD protection and proper grounding.\n","exam":"# Electromagnetics & Antennas: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks. Use c = 2.998 × 10^8 m/s and eta0 = 376.7 Ω.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** In a lossless medium the phase constant is\nA) 2 pi/lambda  B) lambda/(2 pi)  C) 1/lambda  D) c/f\n\n**2.** The Poynting vector gives\nA) the electric field  B) the power density (direction and magnitude of energy flow)  C) the magnetic flux  D) the charge density\n\n**3.** The tangential component of the electric field at a boundary is\nA) zero always  B) discontinuous  C) continuous  D) equal to the normal component\n\n**4.** The skin depth of copper at 1 GHz is about\nA) 2 mm  B) 2 μm  C) 20 μm  D) 20 mm\n\n**5.** Total internal reflection occurs when a wave goes from\nA) a less dense to a denser medium at any angle  B) air to glass  C) a denser to a less dense medium beyond the critical angle  D) a conductor to air\n\n**6.** The velocity on a lossless line with L' = 250 nH/m and C' = 100 pF/m is\nA) 3 × 10^8 m/s  B) 2 × 10^8 m/s  C) 1 × 10^8 m/s  D) 5 × 10^8 m/s\n\n**7.** A VSWR of 1 means\nA) total reflection  B) a short circuit  C) an open circuit  D) a matched load with no reflection\n\n**8.** A load of 25 Ω on a 50 Ω line has VSWR\nA) 1  B) 2  C) 3  D) 0.5\n\n**9.** The input impedance of a line of length lambda/2 equals\nA) Z0  B) the load impedance  C) Z0^2/Z_L  D) zero\n\n**10.** A quarter-wave transformer matching 50 Ω to 200 Ω has Z1 equal to\nA) 100 Ω  B) 125 Ω  C) 150 Ω  D) 70.7 Ω\n\n**11.** A shorted stub of length lambda/8 acts as\nA) a capacitor  B) an inductor (+j Z0)  C) a resistor  D) an open circuit\n\n**12.** Waves below the cutoff frequency of a waveguide\nA) propagate with attenuation  B) propagate faster  C) are evanescent (decay exponentially)  D) turn into TM modes\n\n**13.** The product of phase and group velocity in a waveguide is\nA) c  B) c^2  C) 1  D) zero\n\n**14.** The half-wave dipole has a gain (lossless) of about\nA) 0 dBi  B) 1.76 dBi  C) 2.15 dBi  D) 5 dBi\n\n**15.** The effective aperture of an isotropic antenna is\nA) lambda^2/(4 pi)  B) lambda/(4 pi)  C) 4 pi/lambda^2  D) lambda^2\n\n**16.** The beamwidth of a dish of diameter D at wavelength lambda is about\nA) 70 D/lambda degrees  B) 70 lambda/D degrees  C) lambda D  D) 360 degrees\n\n**17.** The radiation resistance of a short dipole of length l is proportional to\nA) l  B) (l/lambda)^2  C) lambda/l  D) 1/l\n\n**18.** Doubling the number of elements of a uniform broadside array (lambda/2 spacing) raises the directivity by\nA) 1 dB  B) 6 dB  C) 3 dB  D) 10 dB\n\n**19.** Grating lobes in an array appear when the element spacing is\nA) too small  B) larger than about lambda (or too large for the steering angle)  C) zero  D) lambda/4\n\n**20.** The free-space path loss increases by how much when the distance is doubled?\nA) 3 dB  B) 12 dB  C) 20 dB  D) 6 dB\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** Find the free-space wavelength at 2.4 GHz and the velocity, wavelength at 1 GHz and intrinsic impedance in a dielectric with epsilon_r = 9.\n\n**B2. (5)** Find the skin depth of copper at 1 GHz, and of seawater (sigma = 4 S/m) at 1 MHz. Why is a very thin plating enough for a microwave conductor?\n\n**B3. (5)** A 100 Ω load is on a 50 Ω line: |Gamma|, VSWR, return loss and the reflected power for 10 W incident.\n\n**B4. (5)** For a 50 Ω line and a 100 Ω load find Z_in at lambda/8 and the impedance of a quarter-wave transformer matching 50 Ω to 100 Ω.\n\n**B5. (5)** For WR-90 (a = 22.86 mm) find the TE10 cutoff, and at 10 GHz the guide wavelength, phase velocity and group velocity.\n\n**B6. (5)** Give the radiation resistance, directivity (linear and in dBi) and half-power beamwidth of a half-wave dipole.\n\n**B7. (5)** A 1 m dish at 10 GHz has 60 percent aperture efficiency: gain in dBi, beamwidth and effective aperture.\n\n**B8. (5)** Use the Friis equation for a 2.4 GHz link over 100 m with 20 dBm transmit power and 2.15 dBi antennas at both ends.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. Plane waves and boundaries.** (a) For air to a dielectric with epsilon_r = 4 find Gamma, tau and the fraction of power reflected; for air to glass (n = 1.5) the reflected fraction [4]. (b) The Brewster and critical angles of glass [3]. (c) The skin depths of copper at 60 Hz, 1 MHz and 1 GHz, and what they mean for shielding [3].\n\n**C2. Transmission lines.** (a) Z0 and velocity for L' = 250 nH/m, C' = 100 pF/m, and the Z0 of a coax with a = 0.45 mm, b = 1.47 mm, epsilon_r = 2.25 [3]. (b) For loads 100 Ω, 25 Ω, 75 Ω and 50 + j50 Ω on a 50 Ω line: |Gamma|, VSWR and return loss [4]. (c) Single-stub matching of 100 + j50 Ω: the two solutions [3].\n\n**C3. Waveguides.** For WR-90 give the cutoffs of TE10, TE20, TE01 and a table of lambda_g, v_p/c, v_g/c and the wave impedance at 8, 10 and 12 GHz; explain why v_p exceeds c and compare waveguides with coax [10].\n\n**C4. Antennas.** (a) Half-wave dipole: length at 100 MHz and 2.4 GHz, Rr, D, HPBW; the gain with a 5 Ω loss resistance [3]. (b) Short dipoles of lambda/10 and lambda/50: Rr and why they are inefficient [3]. (c) Uniform broadside arrays of 2, 4, 8 and 16 elements: directivity; beam steering and grating lobes [4].\n\n**C5. Links and EMC.** (a) The free-space path loss for 36,000 km at 4 GHz, 1 km at 2.4 GHz and 10 km at 900 MHz [3]. (b) A satellite downlink: 4 GHz, 36,000 km, P_t = 60 dBm, G_t = 40 dBi, G_r = 35 dBi: received power [2]. (c) The power density and the rms field at 100 m for an EIRP of 1 kW [2]. (d) Five EMC design practices [3].\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **A** · 2 **B** · 3 **C** · 4 **B** · 5 **C** · 6 **B** · 7 **D** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **A** · 11 **B** · 12 **C** · 13 **B** · 14 **C** · 15 **A** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **C** · 19 **B** · 20 **D**\n\nBrief reasons: 6. v = 1/sqrt(2.5 × 10^-17) = 2 × 10^8. 8. Gamma = -1/3: VSWR = 2. 10. sqrt(50 × 200) = 100. 11. Z_in = j Z0 tan(pi/4) = j Z0. 14. 1.64 = 2.15 dBi. 18. Doubling N doubles D: +3 dB. 20. 20 log10 2 = 6 dB.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** Step 1: lambda = c/f = 2.998 × 10^8/(2.4 × 10^9) = **12.5 cm**.\nStep 2: dielectric epsilon_r = 9: v = c/3 = **1.0 × 10^8 m/s**.\nStep 3: lambda at 1 GHz = v/f = **10.0 cm**.\nStep 4: eta = 376.7/3 = **125.6 Ω**.\n\n**B2.** Step 1: copper at 1 GHz: delta = 1/sqrt(π f mu0 sigma) = 1/sqrt(π × 10^9 × 4π × 10^-7 × 5.8 × 10^7) = 1/sqrt(2.29 × 10^11) = **2.09 μm**.\nStep 2: seawater at 1 MHz: delta = 1/sqrt(π × 10^6 × 4π × 10^-7 × 4) = **0.25 m**.\nStep 3: at microwave frequencies the current flows only in a layer about 2 μm thick, so a plating of a few skin depths (5 to 10 μm) of a good conductor such as silver or gold on any base metal gives the same conductivity as a solid conductor.\n\n**B3.** Step 1: Gamma = (100 - 50)/(100 + 50) = **0.333**.\nStep 2: VSWR = (1 + 0.333)/(1 - 0.333) = **2.0**.\nStep 3: return loss = 20 log10(3) = **9.54 dB**.\nStep 4: reflected power = 10 × (1/3)² = **1.11 W** (8.89 W delivered).\n\n**B4.** Step 1: Z_in(lambda/8) = 50 (100 + j50)/(50 + j100) = 50 (100 + j50)(50 - j100)/12,500 = 50 (10,000 - j7500)/12,500 = **40 - j30 Ω**.\nStep 2: quarter-wave transformer: Z1 = sqrt(50 × 100) = **70.7 Ω**.\n\n**B5.** Step 1: f_c = c/(2a) = 2.998 × 10^8/0.04572 = **6.557 GHz**.\nStep 2: at 10 GHz: r = sqrt(1 - (6.557/10)²) = sqrt(1 - 0.430) = 0.755.\nStep 3: lambda = 29.98 mm; lambda_g = 29.98/0.755 = **39.7 mm**.\nStep 4: v_p = c/0.755 = **1.324 c**; v_g = 0.755 c (v_p v_g = c²).\n\n**B6.** R_r = **73.1 Ω**; D = **1.641 (2.15 dBi)**; HPBW = **78.1°**.\n\n**B7.** Step 1: lambda = 3.0 cm; G = 0.6 (π × 1/0.02998)² = 0.6 × 10,981 = 6589 = **38.2 dBi**.\nStep 2: HPBW = 70 × 0.02998/1 = **2.1°**.\nStep 3: A_e = 0.6 × π × 1²/4 = **0.471 m²**.\n\n**B8.** Step 1: FSPL = 20 log10(0.1) + 20 log10(2400) + 32.44 = -20 + 67.60 + 32.44 = **80.0 dB**.\nStep 2: P_r = 20 + 2.15 + 2.15 - 80.0 = **-55.7 dBm**.\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a) eta2 = 188.4 Ω: Gamma = (188.4 - 376.7)/(188.4 + 376.7) = **-0.333**; tau = 1 + Gamma = **0.667**; power reflected = Gamma^2 = **11.1 percent**. Air to glass: Gamma = (1 - 1.5)/(1 + 1.5) = -0.2: **4 percent** reflected.\n(b) Brewster angle = arctan(1.5) = **56.3°**; critical angle (glass to air) = arcsin(1/1.5) = **41.8°**.\n(c) delta = 8.53 mm at 60 Hz, **66.1 μm** at 1 MHz, **2.09 μm** at 1 GHz: at power frequencies shielding by thin sheet is ineffective for magnetic fields (use thick, high-permeability metal), but at radio and microwave frequencies even thin foil (many skin depths) attenuates the field strongly by absorption and reflection.\n\n**C2.**\n(a) Z0 = sqrt(250 × 10^-9/100 × 10^-12) = **50 Ω**; v = 1/sqrt(L'C') = **2 × 10^8 m/s (0.667 c)**; coax Z0 = (60/1.5) ln(3.267) = **47.4 Ω**.\n(b) 100 Ω: |Gamma| **0.333**, VSWR **2.0**, RL **9.54 dB**. 25 Ω: **0.333**, **2.0**, **9.54 dB**. 75 Ω: **0.200**, **1.5**, **13.98 dB**. 50 + j50 Ω: **0.447**, **2.62**, **6.99 dB**.\n(c) Step 1: the load 100 + j50 Ω gives y_L = 1/(2 + j) = 0.4 - j0.2 (normalised).\nStep 2: at **d = 0.1988 lambda** from the load the normalised admittance is 1 + j1.00: a shorted shunt stub of length **0.125 lambda** (susceptance -j1) matches.\nStep 3: the second solution: **d = 0.375 lambda**, admittance 1 - j1.00 and a stub of **0.375 lambda**.\n\n**C3.**\nStep 1: WR-90: f_c(TE10) = **6.557 GHz**, TE20 = **13.114 GHz**, TE01 = **14.754 GHz** (single-mode range about 6.6 to 13.1 GHz).\nStep 2: Table (lambda_g = lambda/sqrt(1 - (f_c/f)^2), v_p = c/sqrt(...), v_g = c sqrt(...), Z = eta0/sqrt(...)): **8 GHz**: 65.4 mm, 1.746, 0.573, 657.6 Ω.\nStep 3: **10 GHz**: 39.7 mm, 1.324, 0.755, 499.0 Ω.\nStep 4: **12 GHz**: 29.8 mm, 1.194, 0.838, 449.8 Ω.\nStep 5: **v_p > c** because the phase velocity is the speed at which a phase front sweeps along the guide axis (the wave zigzags between the walls at an angle; the fronts intersect the axis faster than the wave travels); the energy and information travel at **v_g = c^2/v_p < c**.\nStep 6: Waveguide versus coax: waveguide has low loss, high power capability and no radiation, but is bulky, expensive and has a cutoff (no DC, single-mode band); coax is flexible and broadband from DC but has higher loss and lower power limits.\n\n**C4.**\n(a) l = lambda/2: **1.50 m** at 100 MHz, **6.25 cm** at 2.4 GHz; R_r = **73.1 Ω**; D = **1.641 (2.15 dBi)**; HPBW **78.1°**; with a 5 Ω loss: eta = 73/78 = 0.936: G = 1.536 = **1.86 dBi**.\n(b) R_r = 80 pi^2 (l/lambda)^2: **7.9 Ω** at lambda/10 and **0.32 Ω** at lambda/50: the loss resistance (several ohms in the conductor and matching circuit) becomes comparable to or larger than R_r, so efficiency is low; also a large capacitive reactance gives a high Q and narrow bandwidth.\n(c) Step 1: D = N for lambda/2 spacing: **2, 4, 8, 16** (3, 6, 9, 12 dB).\nStep 2: A phase gradient delta between elements steers the main beam to cos(theta_0) = -delta lambda/(2 pi d) electronically.\nStep 3: **Grating lobes** are extra main beams that appear when d is above about lambda (or larger than lambda/(1 + |sin theta_max|) for scanned arrays): keep d <= lambda/2 for wide scan.\n\n**C5.**\n(a) FSPL = 20 log10(d_km) + 20 log10(f_MHz) + 32.44: **195.6 dB** (36,000 km at 4 GHz); **100.0 dB** (1 km at 2.4 GHz); **111.5 dB** (10 km at 900 MHz).\n(b) P_r = 60 + 40 + 35 - 195.6 = **-60.6 dBm**.\n(c) S = 1000/(4 pi 100^2) = **7.96 mW/m^2**; E_rms = sqrt(S eta0) = sqrt(7.96 × 10^-3 × 376.7) = **1.73 V/m**.\n(d) Keep return paths short with a continuous ground plane; shield sensitive circuits and cables; filter (ferrites, LC) power and signal lines at connectors; use differential signalling and twisted pairs; limit edge rates and separate noisy digital from sensitive analog circuitry; decouple supplies near the pins; ESD protection and proper grounding.\n","notes":"# Electromagnetics & Antennas: Lecture Notes\n\nEvery wireless link, every high-speed circuit board and every microwave oven depends on electromagnetic waves. This course covers Maxwell's equations and plane waves, reflection and transmission, transmission lines and impedance matching, rectangular waveguides, and antennas (radiation, directivity, gain, arrays, the Friis equation). All the numerical examples were computed with numpy/scipy (including the numerical integration of antenna patterns), so they are exact for the stated models. Prerequisites: Physics II (Electricity & Magnetism), Calculus II & ODEs (vector calculus), Signals & Systems and Circuit Analysis.\n\n# Chapter 1: Maxwell's Equations and Plane Waves\n\n## 1.1 The equations\nIn differential form (time-harmonic fields use phasors):\n- Gauss: div D = rho; div B = 0.\n- Faraday: curl E = -dB/dt. Ampere-Maxwell: curl H = J + dD/dt.\nwith D = epsilon E, B = mu H, J = sigma E. The displacement-current term dD/dt makes wave propagation possible. For free space epsilon0 = 8.854 × 10^-12 F/m, mu0 = 4 pi × 10^-7 H/m, so the speed of light **c = 1/sqrt(mu0 epsilon0) = 299,792,458 m/s** and the **intrinsic impedance** of free space **eta0 = sqrt(mu0/epsilon0) = 376.73 Ω** (about 120 pi).\n**Boundary conditions**: the tangential components of E and H are continuous across a boundary (H jumps by any surface current); the normal components of D and B are continuous (D jumps by any surface charge). Inside a perfect conductor E = 0, which gives reflection with Gamma = -1.\n\n## 1.2 Uniform plane waves\nIn a lossless medium (epsilon_r, mu_r) a wave with E in the x direction travelling along z is E_x = E0 cos(omega t - beta z), H_y = E_x/eta, with\n- phase velocity v = c/sqrt(epsilon_r mu_r); wavelength lambda = v/f; phase constant beta = 2 pi/lambda = omega/v;\n- intrinsic impedance eta = eta0 sqrt(mu_r/epsilon_r);\n- time-average Poynting vector (power density) S = |E|^2/(2 eta) for a peak amplitude E (or E_rms^2/eta).\nNumbers: wavelength in free space at 1 MHz **299.8 m**, 100 MHz **3.0 m**, 1 GHz **29.98 cm**, 10 GHz **2.998 cm**. In a dielectric with epsilon_r = 4: v = c/2 = 1.5 × 10^8 m/s, eta = 188.4 Ω and the wavelength at 1 GHz is 15.0 cm. A wave with a peak field of 10 V/m carries S = 100/(2 × 376.7) = **0.133 W/m^2** (E_rms = 7.07 V/m).\n**Polarisation**: linear (E stays along one line), circular (two orthogonal components of equal amplitude, 90° apart: GPS and satellite links) and elliptical; a receiving antenna must match the polarisation of the wave (a polarisation mismatch loss).\n\n## 1.3 Waves in conducting media and the skin effect\nIn a good conductor (sigma >> omega epsilon) fields decay as e^(-z/delta) with the **skin depth** **delta = 1/sqrt(pi f mu sigma)**. For copper (sigma = 5.8 × 10^7 S/m): 60 Hz: **8.53 mm**; 1 MHz: **66.1 μm**; 1 GHz: **2.09 μm**; 10 GHz: **0.66 μm**. The surface resistance Rs = 1/(sigma delta) is 8.25 mΩ per square at 1 GHz: high-frequency current flows in a thin surface layer, so the resistance of a wire increases with the square root of frequency and **thin plating of silver or gold** is enough; the same effect makes thin metal foils good shields (aluminium at 1 MHz: 85 μm). **Seawater** (sigma = 4 S/m) has a skin depth of **25 cm at 1 MHz** but 8 m at 1 kHz, which is why submarines use very low frequencies.\n\n## 1.4 Reflection and transmission at a boundary\nFor normal incidence from medium 1 to medium 2: **Gamma = (eta2 - eta1)/(eta2 + eta1)**, **tau = 1 + Gamma**. Air (eta1 = 377) onto a dielectric with epsilon_r = 4 (eta2 = 188.4): Gamma = **-1/3**, tau = 0.667, the fraction of power reflected = Gamma^2 = **11.1 percent**. Air to glass (n = 1.5): Gamma = **-0.2**, **4 percent** of the power is reflected at each surface (hence anti-reflection coatings), transmitted 96 percent. A conductor (eta2 = 0) gives Gamma = -1 (total reflection). At oblique incidence the polarisation matters: the **Brewster angle** (no reflection of parallel polarisation) for glass is arctan(1.5) = **56.3°**; total internal reflection occurs beyond the **critical angle** arcsin(1/1.5) = **41.8°** (basis of optical fibres).\n\n# Chapter 2: Transmission Lines\n\n## 2.1 The model\nTwo conductors carrying a wave are modelled by per-unit-length inductance L' and capacitance C' (and R', G' for losses). For a lossless line: **characteristic impedance Z0 = sqrt(L'/C')**, **velocity v = 1/sqrt(L'C')**. Example: L' = 250 nH/m, C' = 100 pF/m: Z0 = **50 Ω**, v = 2.0 × 10^8 m/s = **0.667 c**. Coaxial cable: Z0 = (60/sqrt(epsilon_r)) ln(b/a): a = 0.45 mm, b = 1.47 mm and polyethylene (epsilon_r = 2.25): Z0 = **47.4 Ω** (close to 50 Ω). A line must be treated as a transmission line (not a simple wire) when its length exceeds about lambda/10.\n\n## 2.2 Reflection, standing waves, matching\nAt a load Z_L: **Gamma_L = (Z_L - Z0)/(Z_L + Z0)**; **VSWR = (1 + |Gamma|)/(1 - |Gamma|)**; **return loss = -20 log10 |Gamma|** dB; **mismatch loss = -10 log10(1 - |Gamma|^2)** dB. For Z0 = 50 Ω:\n```\nload            |Gamma|   phase    VSWR    return loss   mismatch loss\n50              0         -        1.00    infinite      0 dB\n100             0.333     0°       2.00    9.54 dB       0.51 dB\n25              0.333     180°     2.00    9.54 dB       0.51 dB\n75              0.200     0°       1.50    13.98 dB      0.18 dB\n50 + j50        0.447     63.4°    2.62    6.99 dB       0.97 dB\n100 + j50       0.447     26.6°    2.62    6.99 dB       0.97 dB\n```\nOf 10 W forward power into a 100 Ω load on a 50 Ω line, 10 × (1/3)^2 = **1.11 W** is reflected and **8.89 W** delivered. Matched loads (Z_L = Z0) absorb all power and the voltage is the same everywhere; mismatched loads create **standing waves** with voltage maxima where the incident and reflected waves add (V_max/V_min = VSWR).\n**Input impedance**: **Z_in = Z0 (Z_L + j Z0 tan(beta l))/(Z0 + j Z_L tan(beta l))**. For Z0 = 50 and Z_L = 100: at l = lambda/4 Z_in = **25 Ω** (the inversion Z0^2/Z_L); at l = lambda/8 Z_in = **40 - j30 Ω**; at l = lambda/2 Z_in = 100 Ω (repeats). A shorted line of length lambda/8 looks like an inductor of **+j50 Ω**; a short-circuited line of lambda/4 looks like an open circuit (a very high impedance), and an open line of lambda/4 looks like a short.\n\n## 2.3 Matching techniques\n- **Quarter-wave transformer**: a line of length lambda/4 and characteristic impedance **Z1 = sqrt(Z0 Z_L)** matches a real load to the line: 50 Ω to 100 Ω: **70.7 Ω**; 75 Ω to 300 Ω: **150 Ω**; 50 Ω to 200 Ω: 100 Ω. Narrowband (exact at one frequency).\n- **Single-stub matching**: a shorted shunt stub of length l placed at a distance d from the load cancels the reactive part. Example: Z_L = 100 + j50 Ω on a 50 Ω line: at **d = 0.1986 lambda** from the load the normalised admittance is 1 + j1.00, so a short-circuited stub of length **l = 0.125 lambda** (susceptance -j1) matches; the second solution is d = 0.3748 lambda with a stub of 0.375 lambda.\n- **Lumped L-networks**, **tapered lines** (broadband) and the **Smith chart**: a graphical chart of Gamma on which impedance and admittance circles let you read the matching steps. Practical: antennas are matched to 50 Ω with matching networks, baluns and tuners.\n**Other topics**: lossy lines (attenuation in dB/m; coax loss rises with frequency), time-domain reflections (TDR: a step sent down the line reveals discontinuities), pulse reflections on digital PCB traces (terminate lines longer than about 1/6 of the signal rise-time distance), microstrip and stripline.\n\n# Chapter 3: Waveguides\n\nA hollow metal pipe can guide waves above a **cutoff frequency**. For a rectangular guide of width a and height b (a > b) the modes TE_mn and TM_mn have cutoff **f_c = (c/2) sqrt((m/a)^2 + (n/b)^2)** (air-filled). The dominant mode is **TE10** with **f_c = c/(2a)**. Example: standard X-band guide WR-90, a = 22.86 mm, b = 10.16 mm: TE10 cutoff **6.557 GHz**, next modes TE20 at **13.114 GHz** and TE01 at 14.754 GHz, so the single-mode band is about 6.56 to 13.1 GHz (the recommended band is 8.2 to 12.4 GHz).\nAbove cutoff: guide wavelength **lambda_g = lambda/sqrt(1 - (f_c/f)^2)**; phase velocity **v_p = c/sqrt(1 - (f_c/f)^2)** (greater than c!); group velocity **v_g = c sqrt(1 - (f_c/f)^2)** (the speed of energy, less than c), v_p v_g = c^2; wave impedance of TE10 Z = eta0/sqrt(1 - (f_c/f)^2). For WR-90:\n```\nf (GHz)   lambda_g (mm)   v_p/c   v_g/c   Z_TE10 (ohm)\n8         65.4            1.746   0.573   657.6\n10        39.7            1.324   0.755   499.0\n12        29.8            1.194   0.838   449.8\n```\nBelow cutoff the field decays exponentially (**evanescent**): no propagation. Waveguides have very low loss and high power capacity (radar, satellite feeds) but are bulky and band-limited; at lower frequencies coaxial cable is used, and at optical frequencies dielectric waveguides (optical fibre, with total internal reflection). Cavity resonators (microwave ovens: 2.45 GHz) and the power flow in a guide complete the picture.\n\n# Chapter 4: Radiation and Antenna Parameters\n\n## 4.1 How antennas radiate\nAccelerating charges radiate: time-varying currents on an antenna produce waves that detach from it. Regions: **reactive near field** (very close), **radiating near field** (Fresnel) and **far field** (Fraunhofer: beyond **R = 2 D^2/lambda**, where D is the largest antenna dimension), where the wave is locally a plane wave with E and H perpendicular, E/H = eta0, and the field falls as 1/R. The **Hertzian (short) dipole** of length l << lambda has a radiation resistance **R_r = 80 pi^2 (l/lambda)^2**: l = lambda/10: **7.9 Ω**; l = lambda/50: **0.32 Ω** (very inefficient: the loss resistance dominates).\n\n## 4.2 Parameters\n- **Radiation pattern**: the angular distribution of power (usually normalised, in dB), with **main lobe**, **side lobes**, **nulls** and the **half-power beamwidth (HPBW)**.\n- **Directivity D** = maximum radiation intensity / average radiation intensity = 4 pi U_max/P_rad; **gain G = eta D** where **eta = R_r/(R_r + R_loss)** is the radiation efficiency; dBi is relative to an isotropic radiator (D = 1, 0 dBi).\n- **Input impedance**, **VSWR/bandwidth**, **polarisation**.\n- **Effective aperture** **A_e = lambda^2 G/(4 pi)**.\n**Half-wave dipole**: R_r = **73.1 Ω**, pattern F(theta) = [cos((pi/2) cos theta)/sin theta]; numerical integration of the pattern gives **D = 1.641 (2.15 dBi)**, **HPBW = 78.1°**. Hertzian dipole: D = 1.5 (1.76 dBi). With R_r = 73 Ω and a loss resistance of 2 Ω the efficiency is 97.3 percent and the gain **2.03 dBi**.\n\n## 4.3 Aperture antennas\nA parabolic dish of diameter D and aperture efficiency eta_a: **G = eta_a (pi D/lambda)^2**, HPBW ≈ 70 lambda/D degrees, A_e = eta_a pi D^2/4. For D = 1 m at 10 GHz (lambda = 3.0 cm) and eta_a = 0.6: **G = 6589 = 38.2 dBi**, **HPBW = 2.1°**, A_e = **0.471 m^2**; check: lambda^2 G/(4 pi) = 0.471 m^2 ✓. The far-field distance 2 D^2/lambda is **66.7 m**. Horn antennas, patch (microstrip) antennas, Yagi-Uda arrays and helical antennas are other common types.\n\n# Chapter 5: Arrays and Wireless Links\n\n## 5.1 Antenna arrays\nThe pattern of an array = element pattern × **array factor** AF. For N identical elements with spacing d and progressive phase delta: AF = sin(N psi/2)/sin(psi/2), psi = 2 pi d cos(theta)/lambda + delta. A uniform broadside array (delta = 0) with spacing lambda/2 has **directivity D = N** (computed numerically: N = 2: 2.0, 4: 4.0, 8: 8.0, 16: 16.0 for isotropic elements), main-beam narrowing as 1/N; applying a phase gradient **steers the beam** electronically (phased arrays in radar, 5G base stations; MIMO). Spacing larger than lambda gives **grating lobes**.\n\n## 5.2 The Friis transmission equation\n**P_r = P_t G_t G_r (lambda/(4 pi R))^2** or in dB: P_r = P_t + G_t + G_r - FSPL, where free-space path loss FSPL = 20 log10(4 pi R/lambda) (equal to 20 log10 d_km + 20 log10 f_MHz + 32.44). Examples: a Wi-Fi link at 2.4 GHz over 100 m with 20 dBm transmit power and 2.15 dBi antennas at both ends: P_r = **-55.8 dBm**. A geostationary satellite downlink: 4 GHz, R = 36,000 km, P_t = 60 dBm (30 dBW), G_t = 40 dBi, G_r = 35 dBi: P_r = **-60.6 dBm**. **Power density**: for an EIRP of 1 kW (P_t G_t) at 100 m: S = 1000/(4 pi 100^2) = **7.96 mW/m^2**, i.e. E_rms = sqrt(S eta0) = **1.73 V/m** (exposure limits: a few tens of V/m, so this is far below them). The **radar equation** adds the target's cross-section: P_r = P_t G^2 lambda^2 sigma/((4 pi)^3 R^4): the received power falls as R^-4.\n\n## 5.3 Propagation and electromagnetic compatibility\nGround wave (LF/MF), **sky wave** (HF, reflection by the ionosphere), **line-of-sight** (VHF and above; radio horizon about 4.12 (sqrt(h1) + sqrt(h2)) km with heights in metres), multipath fading, diffraction and rain attenuation (above 10 GHz). **EMC**: radiated and conducted emissions and immunity; reduce interference by shielding (skin effect), filtering, grounding, short return paths, twisted pairs, differential signalling, ferrites and layout (continuous ground planes); ESD protection; standards (CISPR, FCC Part 15).\n**Safety**: specific absorption rate (SAR) limits for phones, power density limits for exposure.\n\n## Quick reference\n- c = 3 × 10^8 m/s, eta0 = 377 Ω, lambda = c/f; skin depth delta = 1/sqrt(pi f mu sigma).\n- Gamma = (Z_L - Z0)/(Z_L + Z0); VSWR = (1 + |Gamma|)/(1 - |Gamma|); quarter-wave transformer Z1 = sqrt(Z0 Z_L).\n- Waveguide TE10: f_c = c/(2a); v_p v_g = c^2.\n- Dipole: R_r = 73 Ω, D = 1.64 (2.15 dBi); dish G = eta_a (pi D/lambda)^2; A_e = lambda^2 G/(4 pi).\n- Friis: P_r = P_t + G_t + G_r - 20 log10(4 pi R/lambda); far field R > 2 D^2/lambda.\n","plan":"# Electromagnetics & Antennas: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 10 to 12 hours per week (3 reading, 7 to 8 problems and simulation, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Electromagnetics rewards visualisation: use Python (numpy, matplotlib) to plot fields, patterns and reflection coefficients, and a free simulator for transmission lines and antennas. Optional hardware: a NanoVNA (about 50 dollars) measures impedance, VSWR and the Smith chart of real antennas and cables, and an RTL-SDR shows real signals.\n\n## Textbooks\n- David Cheng, *Field and Wave Electromagnetics*; Fawwaz Ulaby, *Fundamentals of Applied Electromagnetics* (clear, many examples).\n- Matthew Sadiku, *Elements of Electromagnetics*.\n- David Pozar, *Microwave Engineering* (transmission lines, waveguides, matching, Smith chart).\n- Constantine Balanis, *Antenna Theory: Analysis and Design*; John Kraus and Ronald Marhefka, *Antennas for All Applications*.\n- Henry Ott, *Electromagnetic Compatibility Engineering*; Howard Johnson and Martin Graham, *High-Speed Digital Design*.\n- Richard Feynman, *The Feynman Lectures on Physics*, Volume II (physical intuition).\n\n## Free courses and tools\n- MIT OpenCourseWare 6.007 Electromagnetic Energy and 8.02; Walter Lewin's 8.02 lectures; Coursera/edX electromagnetics and antennas courses.\n- Tools: Python (numpy, scipy, matplotlib, scikit-rf for network and Smith-chart work), Smith chart software (Smith V4.1, the Online Smith Chart Tool), openEMS and NEC2 (4nec2) for antennas, FEMM (low frequency fields), Falstad's EM simulations, QUCS and LTspice for transmission lines, a NanoVNA with NanoVNA-Saver.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Vector fields and Maxwell's equations (Ch 1.1).** Do: review div, curl and Stokes' theorem; Assignment 1 Q6 (boundary conditions).\n\n**Week 2: Plane waves in lossless media (Ch 1.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1, Q5, Q7. Plot E and H of a travelling wave in Python.\n\n**Week 3: Waves in conductors, skin effect (Ch 1.3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q2. Compute the skin depth of common metals versus frequency.\n\n**Week 4: Reflection and refraction at boundaries (Ch 1.4).** Do: Assignment 1 Q3, Q4. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week (Chapters 1 to 3.1).\n\n**Week 5: Transmission line theory (Ch 2.1).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1. Derive the telegrapher's equations; simulate a pulse on a mismatched line in LTspice.\n\n**Week 6: Reflection, VSWR, input impedance (Ch 2.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q2 to Q4. Measure the impedance of an antenna or a cable with a NanoVNA.\n\n**Week 7: The Smith chart and matching (Ch 2.3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q5. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam. Design a single-stub match on the Smith chart and verify numerically.\n\n**Week 8: Rectangular waveguides (Ch 3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q6, Q7. Plot the TE10 field pattern and dispersion diagram.\n\n**Week 9: Radiation and antenna parameters (Ch 4.1, 4.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1, Q2. Compute the half-wave dipole pattern and directivity numerically.\n\n**Week 10: Aperture antennas (Ch 4.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q3. Compute dish gain and beamwidth for different diameters and frequencies.\n\n**Week 11: Arrays and beam steering (Ch 5.1).** Do: Assignment 3 Q5. Plot array factors for different spacings and phase gradients; see grating lobes.\n\n**Week 12: Friis, link budgets, EMC (Ch 5.2, 5.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q4, Q6, Q7. Build a link budget spreadsheet for a Wi-Fi and a satellite link.\n\n**Week 13: Project.** Design, simulate and (if possible) build and measure an antenna or a matching network (a dipole or a Yagi for FM or 433 MHz, a quarter-wave ground plane, a patch antenna at 2.4 GHz): compare simulation with VNA measurements and report the resonance, bandwidth, VSWR and gain estimates.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, formula sheet only).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can state Maxwell's equations and boundary conditions and compute plane-wave quantities (wavelength, impedance, power density).\n- [ ] I can compute skin depth and explain shielding and high-frequency resistance.\n- [ ] I can compute reflection and transmission at boundaries, and Brewster and critical angles.\n- [ ] I can analyse transmission lines: Z0, Gamma, VSWR, return loss, Z_in.\n- [ ] I can design quarter-wave and single-stub matching and read a Smith chart.\n- [ ] I can compute waveguide cutoff, guide wavelength, phase and group velocity.\n- [ ] I can compute dipole and dish parameters (directivity, gain, beamwidth, effective aperture).\n- [ ] I can explain arrays, beam steering and grating lobes.\n- [ ] I can use the Friis equation and build a link budget; I know the main EMC practices.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with a formula sheet only.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Draw the picture first: fields, directions, boundaries, equivalent circuits.\n2. Keep units straight, especially wavelength, dB and dBi; check results for orders of magnitude (a dipole is about 0.5 lambda long).\n3. Verify analytic results numerically in Python (patterns, integrals, Smith chart points).\n4. Use physical intuition: standing waves, resonance, why things radiate and why shields work.\n5. Measure real things with a VNA or SDR whenever you can: the theory becomes concrete.\n","test":"# Electromagnetics & Antennas: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Use c = 3 × 10^8 m/s and eta0 = 377 Ω.\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** The intrinsic impedance of free space is about\nA) 50 Ω  B) 75 Ω  C) 377 Ω  D) 1000 Ω\n\n**2.** The speed of an electromagnetic wave in a lossless dielectric with epsilon_r = 4 and mu_r = 1 is\nA) c  B) c/2  C) c/4  D) 2c\n\n**3.** The skin depth of a good conductor\nA) increases with frequency  B) is independent of frequency  C) decreases with frequency  D) is zero\n\n**4.** The reflection coefficient at normal incidence from medium 1 to medium 2 is\nA) (eta2 + eta1)/(eta2 - eta1)  B) (eta2 - eta1)/(eta2 + eta1)  C) eta2/eta1  D) 1 - eta2\n\n**5.** A perfect conductor reflects a normally incident wave with a coefficient of\nA) 0  B) +1  C) -1  D) 0.5\n\n**6.** The Brewster angle is the angle at which\nA) total internal reflection starts  B) there is no reflection for parallel polarisation  C) the wave is absorbed  D) the wave is circularly polarised\n\n**7.** The characteristic impedance of a lossless line is\nA) sqrt(L'C')  B) sqrt(L'/C')  C) L'/C'  D) 1/sqrt(L'C')\n\n**8.** A matched line (Z_L = Z0) has a VSWR of\nA) 0  B) 0.5  C) 1  D) infinity\n\n**9.** A load of 100 Ω on a 50 Ω line has a reflection coefficient magnitude of\nA) 0.2  B) 0.33  C) 0.5  D) 1.0\n\n**10.** A line of length lambda/4 terminated in a short circuit has an input impedance that is\nA) zero  B) Z0  C) very high (open circuit)  D) purely resistive and equal to the load\n\n**11.** A quarter-wave matching transformer between Z0 and Z_L has an impedance\nA) Z0 + Z_L  B) (Z0 + Z_L)/2  C) Z0 Z_L  D) sqrt(Z0 Z_L)\n\n**12.** The TE10 cutoff frequency of a rectangular waveguide of width a is\nA) c/(2a)  B) c/a  C) 2c/a  D) c a/2\n\n**13.** In a waveguide the group velocity is\nA) greater than c  B) equal to c  C) less than c  D) infinite\n\n**14.** The half-wave dipole has a radiation resistance of about\nA) 10 Ω  B) 50 Ω  C) 73 Ω  D) 377 Ω\n\n**15.** The directivity of a half-wave dipole is about\nA) 1.0  B) 1.5  C) 1.64  D) 3.0\n\n**16.** Antenna gain equals directivity multiplied by\nA) the radiation efficiency  B) the VSWR  C) the bandwidth  D) the wavelength\n\n**17.** The effective aperture of an antenna of gain G is\nA) lambda G/(4 pi)  B) lambda^2 G/(4 pi)  C) G/lambda^2  D) 4 pi G/lambda\n\n**18.** The far-field distance of an antenna of largest dimension D is about\nA) D  B) lambda  C) 2 D^2/lambda  D) D^2\n\n**19.** The Friis equation shows the received power falling with distance R as\nA) 1/R  B) 1/R^2  C) 1/R^3  D) 1/R^4\n\n**20.** A uniform broadside array of N isotropic elements spaced lambda/2 apart has a directivity of\nA) 2  B) N  C) N^2  D) 1\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Waves (10 marks)** (a) The wavelength in free space at 5 GHz and in a dielectric with epsilon_r = 9 at 1 GHz, and the intrinsic impedance of that dielectric [4] (b) The skin depth of copper at 100 MHz (1 MHz: 66.1 μm) [2] (c) Gamma and the fraction of power reflected at an air-dielectric (epsilon_r = 4) interface [4]\n\n**B2. Transmission lines (10 marks)** A 75 Ω line is terminated in 150 Ω. (a) |Gamma|, VSWR, return loss [4] (b) The reflected and delivered power for 10 W incident [2] (c) A quarter-wave transformer to match a 75 Ω line to a 300 Ω load: its impedance [2] (d) Z_in of the original load at lambda/8 (using 60 - j45 Ω as a check) [2]\n\n**B3. Waveguide (10 marks)** A rectangular guide has a = 3 cm and b = 1.5 cm. (a) The cutoffs of TE10, TE20 and TE01 [3] (b) The guide wavelength and the phase and group velocity at 6.5 GHz [5] (c) Can a 3 GHz signal propagate? [2]\n\n**B4. Antennas (10 marks)** (a) The gain, HPBW and effective aperture of a 0.6 m dish at 5.8 GHz with 55 percent efficiency [4] (b) The Friis received power for a 1 GHz link of 1 km, 10 dBm, 6 dBi antennas at both ends [3] (c) The power density and rms field at 10 m from a source with 100 W EIRP [3]\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **C** · 2 **B** · 3 **C** · 4 **B** · 5 **C** · 6 **B** · 7 **B** · 8 **C** · 9 **B** · 10 **C** · 11 **D** · 12 **A** · 13 **C** · 14 **C** · 15 **C** · 16 **A** · 17 **B** · 18 **C** · 19 **B** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons: 2. v = c/sqrt(4). 3. delta = 1/sqrt(pi f mu sigma). 9. (100 - 50)/(150) = 1/3. 10. Z_in = Z0^2/Z_L with Z_L = 0 is infinite. 19. Received power is proportional to (lambda/(4 pi R))^2.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Step 1: lambda = c/f = 3 × 10^8/(5 × 10^9) = **6.0 cm**.\nStep 2: for epsilon_r = 9: v = c/sqrt 9 = c/3 = 10^8 m/s, so lambda at 1 GHz = 10^8/10^9 = **10 cm**.\nStep 3: eta = 377/sqrt 9 = 377/3 = **125.7 Ω**.\n(b) delta is proportional to 1/sqrt(f): at 100 MHz (100 times 1 MHz) delta = 66.1 μm/sqrt 100 = 66.1/10 = **6.61 μm**.\n(c) Step 1: eta2 = 377/2 = 188.4 Ω; Gamma = (188.4 - 377)/(188.4 + 377) = -188.6/565.4 = **-0.333**.\nStep 2: the fraction of power reflected = Gamma² = **11.1 percent**.\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Step 1: Gamma = (150 - 75)/(150 + 75) = 75/225 = **0.333**.\nStep 2: VSWR = (1 + 0.333)/(1 - 0.333) = **2.0**.\nStep 3: return loss = -20 log10(0.333) = 20 log10(3) = **9.54 dB**.\n(b) Step 1: reflected power = 10 × (1/3)² = **1.11 W**.\nStep 2: delivered = 10 - 1.11 = **8.89 W**.\n(c) Z1 = sqrt(75 × 300) = **150 Ω** (length lambda/4).\n(d) Step 1: Z_in(lambda/8) = Z0 (Z_L + j Z0)/(Z0 + j Z_L) = 75 (150 + j75)/(75 + j150), since tan 45° = 1.\nStep 2: multiply numerator and denominator by (75 - j150): (150 + j75)(75 - j150) = 11,250 - j22,500 + j5625 + 11,250 = 22,500 - j16,875; the denominator is 75² + 150² = 28,125.\nStep 3: Z_in = 75 × (22,500 - j16,875)/28,125 = **60 - j45 Ω** (|Z_in| = 75: at the eighth-wavelength point the magnitude equals Z0 for this load because the normalised load is 2 and tan 45° = 1).\n\n**B3.**\n(a) Step 1: cutoff f_c = (c/2) sqrt((m/a)² + (n/b)²) (a and b from the question).\nStep 2: TE10: c/(2a) = 3 × 10^8/0.06 = **5.0 GHz**; TE20: c/a = **10.0 GHz**; TE01: c/(2b) = **10.0 GHz**.\n(b) Step 1: f_c/f = 5/6.5 = 0.769; r = sqrt(1 - 0.592) = 0.639.\nStep 2: lambda = c/f = 4.615 cm; lambda_g = 4.615/0.639 = **7.2 cm**.\nStep 3: v_p = c/0.639 = **1.57 c** (4.7 × 10^8 m/s); v_g = 0.639 c = **1.92 × 10^8 m/s**; check v_p v_g = c².\n(c) **No**: 3 GHz is below the cutoff of 5 GHz, so the field decays exponentially (evanescent).\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Step 1: lambda = 5.17 cm; G = eta_a (π D/lambda)² = 0.55 (π × 0.6/0.0517)² = 0.55 × 1329 = **731 (28.6 dBi)**.\nStep 2: HPBW ≈ 70 lambda/D = 70 × 0.0517/0.6 = **6.0°**.\nStep 3: A_e = eta_a π D²/4 = 0.55 × π × 0.36/4 = **0.156 m²**.\n(b) Step 1: FSPL (1 km, 1 GHz) = 20 log10(1) + 20 log10(1000) + 32.44 = 0 + 60 + 32.44 = **92.4 dB**.\nStep 2: P_r = 10 + 6 + 6 - 92.4 = **-70.4 dBm**.\n(c) Step 1: S = 100/(4π × 10²) = 100/1256.6 = **0.0796 W/m²**.\nStep 2: E_rms = sqrt(S eta0) = sqrt(0.0796 × 377) = **5.48 V/m**.\n"},mcq:[{"q":"The intrinsic impedance of free space is about","options":["50 Ω","75 Ω","377 Ω","1000 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The speed of an electromagnetic wave in a lossless dielectric with epsilon_r = 4 and mu_r = 1 is","options":["c","c/2","c/4","2c"],"answer":1,"explain":"v = c/sqrt(4).","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The skin depth of a good conductor","options":["increases with frequency","is independent of frequency","decreases with frequency","is zero"],"answer":2,"explain":"delta = 1/sqrt(pi f mu sigma).","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The reflection coefficient at normal incidence from medium 1 to medium 2 is","options":["(eta2 + eta1)/(eta2 - eta1)","(eta2 - eta1)/(eta2 + eta1)","eta2/eta1","1 - eta2"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A perfect conductor reflects a normally incident wave with a coefficient of","options":["0","+1","-1","0.5"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Brewster angle is the angle at which","options":["total internal reflection starts","there is no reflection for parallel polarisation","the wave is absorbed","the wave is circularly polarised"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The characteristic impedance of a lossless line is","options":["sqrt(L'C')","sqrt(L'/C')","L'/C'","1/sqrt(L'C')"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A matched line (Z_L = Z0) has a VSWR of","options":["0","0.5","1","infinity"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A load of 100 Ω on a 50 Ω line has a reflection coefficient magnitude of","options":["0.2","0.33","0.5","1.0"],"answer":1,"explain":"(100 - 50)/(150) = 1/3.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A line of length lambda/4 terminated in a short circuit has an input impedance that is","options":["zero","Z0","very high (open circuit)","purely resistive and equal to the load"],"answer":2,"explain":"Z_in = Z0^2/Z_L with Z_L = 0 is infinite.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A quarter-wave matching transformer between Z0 and Z_L has an impedance","options":["Z0 + Z_L","(Z0 + Z_L)/2","Z0 Z_L","sqrt(Z0 Z_L)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The TE10 cutoff frequency of a rectangular waveguide of width a is","options":["c/(2a)","c/a","2c/a","c a/2"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a waveguide the group velocity is","options":["greater than c","equal to c","less than c","infinite"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The half-wave dipole has a radiation resistance of about","options":["10 Ω","50 Ω","73 Ω","377 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The directivity of a half-wave dipole is about","options":["1.0","1.5","1.64","3.0"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Antenna gain equals directivity multiplied by","options":["the radiation efficiency","the VSWR","the bandwidth","the wavelength"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The effective aperture of an antenna of gain G is","options":["lambda G/(4 pi)","lambda^2 G/(4 pi)","G/lambda^2","4 pi G/lambda"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The far-field distance of an antenna of largest dimension D is about","options":["D","lambda","2 D^2/lambda","D^2"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Friis equation shows the received power falling with distance R as","options":["1/R","1/R^2","1/R^3","1/R^4"],"answer":1,"explain":"Received power is proportional to (lambda/(4 pi R))^2.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A uniform broadside array of N isotropic elements spaced lambda/2 apart has a directivity of","options":["2","N","N^2","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a lossless medium the phase constant is","options":["2 pi/lambda","lambda/(2 pi)","1/lambda","c/f"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Poynting vector gives","options":["the electric field","the power density (direction and magnitude of energy flow)","the magnetic flux","the charge density"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The tangential component of the electric field at a boundary is","options":["zero always","discontinuous","continuous","equal to the normal component"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The skin depth of copper at 1 GHz is about","options":["2 mm","2 μm","20 μm","20 mm"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Total internal reflection occurs when a wave goes from","options":["a less dense to a denser medium at any angle","air to glass","a denser to a less dense medium beyond the critical angle","a conductor to air"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The velocity on a lossless line with L' = 250 nH/m and C' = 100 pF/m is","options":["3 × 10^8 m/s","2 × 10^8 m/s","1 × 10^8 m/s","5 × 10^8 m/s"],"answer":1,"explain":"v = 1/sqrt(2.5 × 10^-17) = 2 × 10^8.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A VSWR of 1 means","options":["total reflection","a short circuit","an open circuit","a matched load with no reflection"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A load of 25 Ω on a 50 Ω line has VSWR","options":["1","2","3","0.5"],"answer":1,"explain":"Gamma = -1/3: VSWR = 2.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The input impedance of a line of length lambda/2 equals","options":["Z0","the load impedance","Z0^2/Z_L","zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A quarter-wave transformer matching 50 Ω to 200 Ω has Z1 equal to","options":["100 Ω","125 Ω","150 Ω","70.7 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"sqrt(50 × 200) = 100.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A shorted stub of length lambda/8 acts as","options":["a capacitor","an inductor (+j Z0)","a resistor","an open circuit"],"answer":1,"explain":"Z_in = j Z0 tan(pi/4) = j Z0.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Waves below the cutoff frequency of a waveguide","options":["propagate with attenuation","propagate faster","are evanescent (decay exponentially)","turn into TM modes"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The product of phase and group velocity in a waveguide is","options":["c","c^2","1","zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The half-wave dipole has a gain (lossless) of about","options":["0 dBi","1.76 dBi","2.15 dBi","5 dBi"],"answer":2,"explain":"1.64 = 2.15 dBi.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The effective aperture of an isotropic antenna is","options":["lambda^2/(4 pi)","lambda/(4 pi)","4 pi/lambda^2","lambda^2"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The beamwidth of a dish of diameter D at wavelength lambda is about","options":["70 D/lambda degrees","70 lambda/D degrees","lambda D","360 degrees"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The radiation resistance of a short dipole of length l is proportional to","options":["l","(l/lambda)^2","lambda/l","1/l"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Doubling the number of elements of a uniform broadside array (lambda/2 spacing) raises the directivity by","options":["1 dB","6 dB","3 dB","10 dB"],"answer":2,"explain":"Doubling N doubles D: +3 dB.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Grating lobes in an array appear when the element spacing is","options":["too small","larger than about lambda (or too large for the steering angle)","zero","lambda/4"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The free-space path loss increases by how much when the distance is doubled?","options":["3 dB","12 dB","20 dB","6 dB"],"answer":3,"explain":"20 log10 2 = 6 dB.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
